From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Julien Etienne <julien.etienne@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 15:07:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 363/366] Input: matrix_keypad: leave columns inactive between
 polled scans

After each scan the driver drives every column active. That lets any
key press pull a row low and raise a row interrupt. Polling mode
requests no row interrupts, so the state serves no purpose there. It
still costs a write per column after the scan and another before the
next one. Behind an I2C expander such as the Gemini PDA's AW9523, the
Gemini's seven columns cost 14 bus writes per scan, or 700 a second at
a 20 ms interval.

In polling mode, leave the columns in their inactive state after the
scan. The next scan's deactivation then finds them already inactive,
and a caching GPIO provider skips those writes. Interrupt mode is
unchanged.

This refines the optional polling mode added earlier in this series.
Upstream, it belongs in that patch.

Compile-tested only; not yet run on hardware.
---
 drivers/input/keyboard/matrix_keypad.c | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/input/keyboard/matrix_keypad.c b/drivers/input/keyboard/matrix_keypad.c
index 03c3f5839..f15811231 100644
--- a/drivers/input/keyboard/matrix_keypad.c
+++ b/drivers/input/keyboard/matrix_keypad.c
@@ -192,9 +192,12 @@ static void matrix_keypad_scan(struct work_struct *work)
 	memcpy(keypad->last_key_state, new_state, sizeof(new_state));
 
 out:
-	activate_all_cols(keypad, true);
-
 	if (keypad->poll_interval_ms) {
+		/*
+		 * Columns are only held active between scans so that a key
+		 * press can raise a row interrupt. Polling needs no such
+		 * interrupt, so leave them inactive until the next scan.
+		 */
 		scoped_guard(spinlock_irq, &keypad->lock) {
 			keypad->scan_pending = false;
 			if (!keypad->stopped)
@@ -205,6 +208,8 @@ static void matrix_keypad_scan(struct work_struct *work)
 		return;
 	}
 
+	activate_all_cols(keypad, true);
+
 	/* Enable IRQs again */
 	scoped_guard(spinlock_irq, &keypad->lock) {
 		keypad->scan_pending = false;
